  "text": " you open your feed for something new. Somehow, you keep returning to the same topics. The videos change, the interests we explore can stay surprisingly familiar. We began to worry that our interests were narrowing without us noticing. Research gave us a reason to take that question seriously. A Spotify study linked algorithmic listening with lower consumption diversity. Other researchers showed, in simulations, how recommendation feedback loops can make users choices more similar and reduce the value they get. That raised a question for us. How can we make unfamiliar interests easier to discover? This is otherwise. Other, for another perspective, wise, for more thoughtful judgment, and an invitation to think otherwise. Start with an interest you choose. Otherwise suggests unfamiliar but related topics across fields. You control how far to explore, from nearby ideas to broader connections. Follow a topic that catches your attention, save it when you want to pursue it and explore from that new starting point. A galaxy map makes your exploration visible so you can revisit discoveries and choose where to go next. Existing research gives this direction an initial basis. In a small user study, oralist produced more useful, previously unknown recommendations. Its authors also proposed letting users adjust the level of novelty. P-U-R-S showed how unexpected recommendations could remain relevant. Our proposed contribution is to bring these ideas into a general topic explorer. Interests you confirm, gradual exploration across fields, and a visual record of your journey. We're developing it as both a product and a research project. Next, we want to test whether a new discovery today can grow into a lasting interest. If your research connects with recommendation, learning, or human computer interaction, we love your guidance. Otherwise, your world, a little wider.",
